      "text": "To determine which of the five segments shown in the figure is the longest, we need to analyze the given angles and the relationships between the segments. Let's denote the segments and angles as follows:\n\n- \\( AB = 5 \\)\n- \\( BC = 4 \\)\n- \\( CD = 3 \\)\n- \\( DA = 2 \\)\n- \\( \\angle BAC = 55^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle CAD = 40^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle BAD = 75^\\circ \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the measure of \\( \\angle ADB \\). Since \\( \\angle BAD = 75^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle BAC = 55^\\circ \\), we have:\n\\[ \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAD - \\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - 75^\\circ - 55^\\circ = 50^\\circ. \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the length of \\( AD \\). We can use the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ABD \\):\n\\[ \\frac{BD}{\\sin \\angle ADB} = \\frac{AD}{\\sin \\angle BAD}. \\]\nWe need to find \\( BD \\). Using the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[ \\frac{BC}{\\sin \\angle BAC} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle ABC}. \\]\nWe know \\( BC = 4 \\), \\( \\angle BAC = 55^\\circ \\), and \\( AB = 5 \\). Let \\( \\angle ABC = \\beta \\). Then:\n\\[ \\frac{4}{\\sin 55^\\circ} = \\frac{5}{\\sin \\beta}. \\]\nSolving for \\( \\sin \\beta \\):\n\\[ \\sin \\beta = \\frac{5 \\sin 55^\\circ}{4}. \\]\nUsing a calculator to find \\( \\sin 55^\\circ \\approx 0.8192 \\):\n\\[ \\sin \\beta \\approx \\frac{5 \\times 0.8192}{4} \\approx 1.023. \\]\nThis value is not possible for a sine function, so we need to re-evaluate the angles and segments. Instead, we can use the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ACD \\):\n\\[ \\frac{CD}{\\sin \\angle CAD} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ACD}. \\]\nWe know \\( CD = 3 \\), \\( \\angle CAD = 40^\\circ \\), and we need to find \\( AC \\) and \\( \\angle ACD \\). First, we find \\( \\angle ACD \\):\n\\[ \\angle ACD = 180^\\circ - \\angle CAD - \\angle CAD = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 40^\\circ = 100^\\circ. \\]\nUsing the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ACD \\):\n\\[ \\frac{CD}{\\sin \\angle CAD} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ADC}. \\]\nSince \\( \\angle ADC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ADB - \\angle DAC = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ - 10^\\circ = 120^\\circ \\) (because \\( \\angle DAC = 10^\\circ \\) is the remaining angle in triangle \\( ADC \\)):\n\\[ \\frac{3}{\\sin 40^\\circ} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin 120^\\circ}. \\]\nUsing a calculator to find \\( \\sin 40^\\circ \\approx 0.6428 \\) and \\( \\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 0.866 \\):\n\\[ \\frac{3}{0.6428} \\approx 4.645 = \\frac{AC}{0.866}. \\]\nSolving for \\( AC \\):\n\\[ AC \\approx 4.645 \\times 0.866 \\approx 4.00. \\]\nNow, using the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ACD \\):\n\\[ \\frac{CD}{\\sin 40^\\circ} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ADC}. \\]\nSince \\( \\angle ADC = 120^\\circ \\):\n\\[ \\frac{3}{0.6428} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin 120^\\circ} = \\frac{AC}{0.866}. \\]\nThis confirms our calculations. Therefore, the length of \\( AD \\) is approximately 4.00, the length of \\( AB \\) is 5, the length of \\( BC \\) is 4, the length of \\( CD \\) is 3, and the length of \\( DA \\) is 2.\n\nThus, the longest segment is \\( \\boxed{AB} \\).",
