      "text": "To determine which of the five segments drawn in the figure is the longest, we need to analyze the angles and the relationships between the segments. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the given angles and segments:**\n   - The angles are \\(55^\\circ\\) at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), and \\(40^\\circ\\) at points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n   - The segments are \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), \\(CD\\), \\(DA\\), and \\(AC\\).\n\n2. **Understand the geometric relationships:**\n   - Since the figure is not to scale, we can use the properties of angles and segments in a circle or triangle to help us determine which segment is the longest.\n   - The angle at point \\(B\\) (which is \\(55^\\circ\\)) is an angle inscribed in a semicircle, so the arc \\(AB\\) is a semicircle. This means the length of arc \\(AB\\) is equal to the length of the radius of the circle.\n   - The angle at point \\(C\\) (which is \\(40^\\circ\\)) is an angle inscribed in a semicircle that excludes arc \\(CD\\). This means the arc \\(CD\\) must be shorter than the semicircle arc \\(AC\\).\n   - The angle at point \\(D\\) (which is \\(75^\\circ\\)) is an angle inscribed in a semicircle that excludes arc \\(AB\\). This means the arc \\(AB\\) is shorter than the semicircle arc \\(DA\\).\n\n3. **Determine the lengths of the segments:**\n   - Since the segments \\(AC\\) and \\(DA\\) are both parts of the semicircles and the length of a chord in a semicircle is equal to the radius of the semicircle, and the lengths of the arcs \\(AB\\), \\(CD\\), and \\(DA\\) are the same as the lengths of the chords that subtend the same angles at the center (which is equal to the length of the radius), we can conclude that the longest segment is the one that is the radius of the semicircle.\n   - Therefore, the longest segment is \\(AC\\).\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{AC}\\).",
